# Building and deploying {{ __node_setup_name }}

This directory was written by `ansible-playbook acme.infra.node_setup`. Running that
again from the project root replaces this guide with the one of the installed
acme.infra and leaves the other files alone: they are yours.

Run every command below **from this directory**, on a machine with ansible-core
and the acme.infra collection installed (see acme.infra's README).

| File | What it is | In git |
|---|---|---|
| `ansible.cfg` | Points Ansible at `inventory.yml` | Yes |
| `inventory.yml` | The servers to deploy to | Yes |
| `group_vars/all.yml` | The settings: download sources, port, directories, account, and more | Yes |
| `prebuild.yml` | Installs node_modules for development, in a throwaway container | Yes |
| `image.yml`, `deploy.yml`, `site.yml`, `remove.yml` | The playbooks: build, deploy, both in one go, remove | Yes |
| `templates/service.container.j2` | The Quadlet file: how Podman and systemd run the service | Yes |
| `container.env` | The container's settings and secrets, first copied from `../.env.production` if the project has one | **No** |
| `images/` | The built images | No |

Only this guide names the project; the other files fit any project and take
its name and version from package.json whenever they run.

## 1. Build the image

```sh
ansible-playbook image.yml
```

This needs Podman 3.4 or newer (RHEL 9 or Ubuntu 22.04) and access to the
download sources below. The image lands in `images/` as
`{{ __node_setup_service }}-<version>.tar`, with the version from package.json. What the project
needs (the `build` and `start:prod` scripts, an optional `.nvmrc`) and the
build options: `ansible-doc -t role acme.infra.node_image`. This directory,
`config/` and `.env` files never go into the image.

Before building, check that everything the app imports is in `dependencies`,
and not in `devDependencies` as well: the image installs `devDependencies` for
the build only, and npm counts a package in both lists as a devDependency (the
build stops when it finds one). `npm ci --omit=dev && npm run start:prod` shows
a missing one ("Cannot find module") on your machine.

## 2. Configure

- `container.env`: the environment variables the app gets, one `KEY=value` per
  line, without quotes (Podman would pass them on). If the project has a
  `.env.production`, the setup copied it here without the quotes. If it sets
  `PORT`, set `node_deploy_container_port` in `group_vars/all.yml` to the same port.
- `../config/production/*.json`: the app's config files. The deploy copies them
  to the server, where the app finds them read-only in `config/`, next to it.
- `group_vars/all.yml`: the port on the server, the service's directory, its
  account and more. All are commented out at their defaults.
- `templates/service.container.j2`: the Quadlet file, for anything else, e.g. a
  memory limit (`PodmanArgs=--memory=512m`). The deploy fills in the names and
  the version.
- `inventory.yml`: the servers. It lists this machine by default.

## 3. Deploy

```sh
ansible-playbook deploy.yml              # add --ask-become-pass if sudo asks for a password
```

The deploy works as root (through sudo) and, on each server:

1. creates the account `{{ __node_setup_service }}` the service runs as, and lets it run
   services while nobody is logged in (`loginctl enable-linger`);
2. copies the image to `/opt/containers/images/` and loads it into that account's Podman;
3. creates `/opt/{{ __node_setup_service }}/`, the service's directory, mounted at `/data`
   in the container and owned by the account, and copies the config files to its
   `config/` directory (removing files the project no longer has);
4. installs `container.env` and the Quadlet file `{{ __node_setup_service }}.container` in
   `/etc/containers/systemd/users/<the account's UID>/`;
5. starts the service, or restarts it when something changed, and waits until it
   answers on its port (`127.0.0.1:3000` by default). If it does not, it shows the service's log.

The app can write only to `/data` (the service's directory, kept across
deploys) and `/tmp`; the rest of the container is read-only. The service starts
again after a reboot. What a server needs: RHEL 9.2 or newer
with Podman (`sudo dnf install podman`), and SSH access with sudo (or as root)
for Ansible. Nothing is downloaded: the image comes from this directory.

## 4. Check it

On the server:

```sh
sudo systemctl --user -M {{ __node_setup_service }}@ status {{ __node_setup_service }}
sudo journalctl _SYSTEMD_USER_UNIT={{ __node_setup_service }}.service
curl http://127.0.0.1:3000/
```

## Deploying a new version

Raise the `version` in package.json, then build and deploy again:

```sh
ansible-playbook site.yml                # image.yml, then deploy.yml
```

The deploy loads the new image, restarts the service and waits until it answers.
It keeps only the version it deployed: other versions' archives in
`/opt/containers/images/` and the account's unused images are removed. A change
to `container.env`, the config files, `group_vars/all.yml` or the Quadlet
template alone needs only `ansible-playbook deploy.yml`.

## Removing it

```sh
ansible-playbook remove.yml              # add --ask-become-pass if sudo asks for a password
```

This removes everything the deploy created on each server: the service, its
account (with its home directory and images), the Quadlet and environment files,
the image archives and `/opt/{{ __node_setup_service }}/`, **the app's data included**. Copy that
directory first if you need it. A deploy afterwards starts from scratch.

## node_modules for development

```sh
ansible-playbook prebuild.yml
```

Installing dependencies runs code from every package (install scripts), so
this runs npm in a throwaway container of the image build's builder image
instead of on this machine. The container gets `package.json`,
`package-lock.json` and `.npmrc`, nothing else, and is removed afterwards. Only
once the install has succeeded, the current `node_modules` is saved as
`node_modules.<UTC time>.bak.tgz` and replaced. To go back:

```sh
cd .. && rm -rf node_modules && tar -xzf node_modules.<UTC time>.bak.tgz
```

The backups stay until you delete them; keep them out of git (`*.tgz` or
`node_modules.*.bak.tgz` in the project's `.gitignore`). The result fits Linux
machines with the same Node.js version as `.nvmrc`.

## Download sources

What `prebuild.yml` and `image.yml` download, and where to point each one in an
offline environment. `deploy.yml` downloads nothing.

| What | From | Change it with |
|---|---|---|
| Red Hat's Node.js images | `registry.access.redhat.com` | `node_image_registry` in `group_vars/all.yml` |
| npm packages | npm's registry | `registry=` in the project's `.npmrc`, which npm uses for all its settings |
| Node.js headers, to compile native modules | `https://nodejs.org/dist` | `node_image_node_dist_url` in `group_vars/all.yml` |
| RPMs in `node_image_build_packages` and `node_image_runtime_packages`, if any | Red Hat's UBI repositories | Base images of your own, set up with your repositories |

Some packages download prebuilt binaries in their install scripts (`sqlite3`
from GitHub, for example); without access they compile from source, which
needs only the Node.js headers.

## In a pipeline

A pipeline runs the same commands. Keep the steps that run your code apart from
the deploy secrets:

```sh
# Test job: no deploy secrets, no SSH keys.
npm ci && npm run test

# Deploy job, from the ansible/ directory. The runner needs Podman, ansible-core and SSH access.
ansible-galaxy collection install --force <the acme.infra tarball this project uses>
ansible-playbook image.yml
(umask 077 && printf '%s\n' "$CONTAINER_ENV" > container.env)   # from a secret variable
ansible-playbook deploy.yml
```

## Updating acme.infra

Install the newer release, then run `ansible-playbook acme.infra.node_setup`
again from the project root to get its guide, and any file this directory does
not have yet. To compare one of your files with the newest defaults, move it
away and run the setup: it writes a fresh copy.
